# Design Principles

Structural and architectural rules for all code. Covers SOLID, cohesion, coupling, and error handling.

## SOLID

- **S — Single Responsibility:** A module or class should have only one reason to change. One actor, one concern.
- **O — Open/Closed:** Extend behavior by adding new code; never modify existing lower layers to support new behavior.
- **L — Liskov Substitution:** Subtypes must be substitutable for their base type without breaking correctness.
- **I — Interface Segregation:** Many small, client-specific interfaces are better than one large general-purpose interface.
- **D — Dependency Inversion:** Depend on abstractions, not on concrete implementations. Expose a plug, never wire directly.

## Cohesion

- Target **functional cohesion**: all elements of a module serve one single well-defined task.
- Never create `Utils`, `Helpers`, or `Common` catch-all modules — that is coincidental cohesion, the worst level.
- High cohesion and low coupling always go together. You cannot have one without the other.
- When writing business rules, communicational cohesion (multiple operations on the same data) is acceptable — if it is a conscious decision, not drift.

## Deletability and coupling

- Break code apart by what it does **not** share with the rest, not by common functionality.
- Keep hard-to-delete code (stable libraries, protocols) far from easy-to-delete code (business logic). Never entangle them.
- Each hard problem should be handled by only one module. If a module does two things, it is usually because changing one requires changing the other — split it.
- Design so parts can be deleted without rewriting the rest. If deleting a module forces rewrites elsewhere, coupling is too tight.

## Composition over inheritance

- Prefer **contain-and-delegate** over class inheritance.
- Never rely on the internal implementation of a base class — that is a white-box dependency and will break.
- Keep inheritance hierarchies shallow. Deep hierarchies drag in the whole tree when you need one leaf.
- Use interface-based polymorphism.

## Error handling at the boundary

- Handle errors at the **outer layers** (end-to-end principle).
- If every layer handles the same error, the inner handling adds no value and obscures the source.
- Validate at system boundaries: user input, external APIs, I/O. Trust internal code and framework guarantees.

## Naming as a continuous act

- Rename as your understanding of a concept evolves. A name is a claim about meaning — update it when the claim changes.
- Good names make the program self-documenting; stale names add accidental complexity.
